    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `get_seeder_and_restorer`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `get_seeder_and_restorer` function is designed to manage the seeding and state restoration of random number generators across different libraries, primarily focusing on NumPy. It accepts a single parameter, `seed`, which must be an integer between 0 and 2^32 - 1. This function returns a tuple containing two callable functions: `seed_all` and `restore_all`. The `seed_all` function seeds all managed random number generators with the provided seed, while `restore_all` restores their states to what they were before seeding. The function relies on a global dictionary `RANDOMS_TO_MANAGE` to keep track of the random number generators it manages. If NumPy is imported, it ensures that a `NumpyRandomWrapper` instance is created and stored globally to prevent garbage collection. The function asserts that `seed_all` is only called when no states are currently being managed, ensuring that states are not overwritten. It handles errors by asserting that the seed is within the valid range and that `seed_all` is not called when states are already being managed. The side effects include modifying the global state of random number generators and potentially creating global variables.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
